course: dictionary
lesson: 33
title: Words that hurt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Abusive is an adjective, a describing word. It describes words or behaviour that are rude, cruel or hurtful, and it is never okay. «Under the Laws of the Game, arguing with the referee can bring a yellow card, abusive language a red card, and the team then plays with one fewer.»
explain: Look at the clues: yellow card, red card, one fewer player. The rule is harsher for abusive language than for arguing, so abusive must mean something far worse than being cross. Ask yourself what word would fit: rude or insulting would fit, so abusive means using rude, insulting or cruel words. Swap that meaning into the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: Abusive can also describe how someone treats another person. An abusive relationship means someone hurts another person again and again. If anyone is abusive to you, tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Abusive is an adjective, so it describes words, behaviour or relationships.
fact: «Abusive» means using rude, insulting or cruel words.
fact: «Abusive» can also mean treating someone cruelly or violently, or using something wrongly or unfairly, especially power.
fact: Phrases you will meet are «abusive language», «abusive messages» and «abusive behaviour».
fact: Nobody has the right to be abusive to you. Tell a trusted adult, and online you can report and block abusive messages.
exercise: «Under the Laws of the Game, arguing with the referee can bring a yellow card, abusive language a red card.» What does abusive language mean? | Words about the weather | Words whispered very quietly | Kind and friendly words | *Rude, insulting words
exercise: If someone is abusive to you, what should you do? | Reply with something even more cruel | Wait and hope it stops by itself | *Tell a trusted adult straight away | Ignore it and say nothing to anyone
exercise: Which phrase describes someone hurting another person again and again? | a sunny afternoon at the park | a quick drink of water | *an abusive relationship | a cheerful greeting
test: Which word describes words that are rude and insulting? | gentle | calm | *abusive | curious
test: Online, what can you do with abusive messages? | share them with friends | ignore them and forget them | *report and block them | copy them to everyone
exam: Which sentence shows abusive behaviour? | Ava shared her crisps with her friend. | Mia read a book in the library. | *Jack called his classmate cruel names every day. | Sam helped carry the bags home.
